\title{Minimum Uncertainty Is a Subgroup: Rigidity of Finite Poisson
Summation and the Extremals of the Donoho--Stark Uncertainty Principle}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{19 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $G$ be a finite abelian group of order $N$ with Pontryagin dual $\Gh$, and let
$f \mapsto \hat f$ denote the discrete Fourier transform. Two classical ``if'' statements
govern the interaction between the transform and the subgroup lattice of $G$: subgroup
indicators attain equality in the Donoho--Stark uncertainty principle
$|\supp f|\cdot|\supp \hat f| \ge N$, and every subgroup $H \le G$ supports a Poisson
summation formula $N\sum_{x \in H} f = |H| \sum_{\psi \in H^{\perp}} \hat f$.
We prove the converses of both and show that the two rigidity theorems describe one and the
same object. First, if a nonempty finite $S \subseteq G$ and a finite $T \subseteq \Gh$
satisfy the Poisson identity for \emph{all} test functions, then $S$ is a subgroup and $T$ is
exactly its annihilator; moreover the identity need only be verified on the $N$ Dirac
functions. Second, a nonzero $f : G \to \C$ satisfies $|\supp f|\cdot|\supp\hat f| = N$ if and
only if $f = c\,\chi\,\1_{a+K}$ for a subgroup $K$, a character $\chi$, a representative $a$
and a scalar $c \ne 0$; the subgroup is unique and equals the difference set
$\supp f - \supp f$. We deduce an arithmetic theory of the extremal class: Lagrange rigidity
of the support sizes, an explicit gap $N + (s - N \bmod s)$ in the uncertainty inequality when
$s = |\supp f| \nmid N$, and the extremal spectrum theorem, which identifies the achievable
support sizes with the divisors of $N$ on an arbitrary finite abelian group. Consequences
include detection of primality by the extremal class, reconstruction of the group order from
the spectrum, closure of the extremal class under products, convolutions, convolution powers
and the Fourier transform, and the identification of the extremal probability distributions
with the uniform distributions on cosets.
\end{abstract}

